=== layout: two-panels

# Cellulosic Ethanol

@left

- biofuel produced from wood, grasses, or the non-edible parts of plants
- Lignocellulose – composed of cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin

@right

- cellulose – crystalline, strong, and resistant to hydrolysis
- hemicellulose –  random, amorphous structure with [little]{underline=true} strength
- ligin – (_next slide_)

@notes

Source: lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 26.

=== layout: two-panels

# Lignin

@left

- part of the secondary cell walls of plants
- 2nd most abundant polymer (cellulose is 1st)
- lacks a defined primary structure
- provides additional strength to trees

@right

![Slide image 23](assets/lect06c-set_4_tp_chapter_12_16_material/image_023.png)

@notes

Source: lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 27.

=== layout: two-panels

# Cellulosic Methods

@left

- Cellulolysis processes
  - use enzymes to break down cellulose into glucose
  - fermentation into alcohols – _same as before_

@right

- Gasification
  - Reacting materials at high temperatures (>700 °C), without combustion
    - with a controlled amount of oxygen and steam.
  - The resulting gas mixture is a fuel

@notes

Source: lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 28.

