=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 8: glucose monitoring

@body

- A finger-stick meter measures glucose in a blood sample.
- Continuous glucose monitors can measure glucose in interstitial fluid.
- A sensor beneath the skin is different from a fully noninvasive measurement.
- Ask what is measured, where, and how accuracy is validated.

[FDA: glucose-measurement devices](https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/safety-communications/do-not-use-smartwatches-or-smart-rings-measure-blood-glucose-levels-fda-safety-communication)

@notes

Source: lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 56.
Converted layout or wording reviewed against the original slide. Original text and import diagnostics are retained in the review evidence.

=== layout: one-panel

# Compare measurement approaches

@body

| Approach | Key distinction |
| --- | --- |
| Blood sample | Direct sample of blood |
| Sensor beneath skin | Interstitial-fluid measurement |
| Proposed tears or optical method | Needs evidence for the intended measurement |
| Watch displaying CGM data | Display is separate from the sensor |

@notes

Source: lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 57.
Converted layout or wording reviewed against the original slide. Original text and import diagnostics are retained in the review evidence.

